# kit:not-a-real-app
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOSTED KIT — the app James described on kit#88
#
# ✅ UPDATE 2026-10-09 (claude-code-bot#105): most of this now EXISTS — it is the
# deployed C# server. `kit-hosted.tests.json` maps the behaviours a test asserts,
# BEH-PULL-1..3 are `pending`, and `check.js kit-hosted` lists the rest by id.
# The paragraphs below are the 2026-09-30 reasoning, kept as written.
#
# James, kit#88 (2026-09-27), after the paragraphs describing the workflow:
#
#   "Also, just an additional note, when I am describing user workflows as I
#   have above, you can add these in kits beh since I am effectively descriving
#   how we should enhance the spec right."
#
# Yes. So this file is that paragraph of his, and the four before it, rewritten
# as behaviours instead of left as prose on an issue. It is the thing he asked
# for one line later: "Ideally I want to be driving the development of kit in
# kit."
#
# ⚠️ EVERY BEHAVIOUR HERE DESCRIBES SOFTWARE THAT HAS NOT BEEN WRITTEN. That is
# the point of writing it down first, and it has two consequences a reader must
# not mistake for defects:
#
#   1. **No test names any of these**, so `node check.js kit-hosted --repo .`
#      exits 1. That red is the WORK ITEM, not a failure of the corpus — it is
#      `BEH-GATE-1` in kit.beh doing exactly what it says. Measured on kit#88
#      (2026-09-30) on a throwaway copy before this file was written.
#   2. **The nouns are deliberately unbound.** There is no `kit-hosted.bindings.json`
#      beside this file, so generation reports `marker:Ungenerated` rather than
#      emitting Playwright. That is not an oversight to be fixed: every bindings
#      file in this directory holds itself to "read off the running app, not
#      invented", and there is no running app to read. Inventing locators for
#      screens that do not exist is the one thing that rule forbids.
#
# ── Why `# kit:not-a-real-app`, and why that is the honest directive ─────────
# saturation.js excludes this corpus from the binding-saturation study, whose
# claim is about how REAL apps reuse nouns. Software that does not exist cannot
# be evidence for it. `# kit:no-ui` would arguably also be true of the backend
# half — `request:*`, `branch:*` and `status:*` will never bind to a locator —
# but the frontend half genuinely is a browser surface, so declaring no-ui
# would be a lie about this file, and existence is the axis that actually
# disqualifies it. One directive, the true one.
#
# 🔴 **THAT LINE IS LOAD-BEARING FOR CI, AND NOT FOR THE REASON IT WAS CHOSEN.**
# Measured on 2026-09-30 with this file's directive deleted and nothing else
# changed: `node kit.test.js` goes from **351 passed, 0 failed** to **350 passed,
# 1 failed**. The failing test is "--check goes RED when the write-up drifts from
# the corpora" — saturation.js reads `behaviours/*.beh` as its POPULATION, so an
# undeclared corpus joins the study and the frozen write-up stops matching.
#
# Two things follow, and the second is why this paragraph exists:
#   * The directive was picked because it is TRUE, and it happens to also be the
#     thing keeping the branch green. Do not read the green as evidence that
#     adding a corpus is free.
#   * **The red does not name its cause.** It says a write-up drifted; it does
#     not say someone added a file. A future wake adding an eleventh corpus will
#     see a saturation test fail on a change that never touched saturation. The
#     fix is a directive or a refreshed write-up, never "revert the corpus".
#
# ── Why this is a THIRD Kit corpus and not more of the other two ────────────
# `kit.beh` closes with "Every behaviour below is real, and every one is already
# tested in `kit.test.js`". Putting unbuilt behaviours there would falsify that
# sentence AND turn kit's own CI gate red, since `kit` is the corpus CI checks
# by name. Its separate warning — do not add browser behaviours to move its 0 —
# points the same way.
#
# `kit-ui.beh` describes the LOCAL browser UI, which exists, and records that 5
# of its 6 generated tests pass against the running app. Unbuilt behaviours
# would cost that claim its meaning too.
#
# So Kit is three apps in its own registry: the command-line tool (kit.beh, no
# UI), the local UI (kit-ui.beh, built), and the hosted service (this file, not
# built).
#
# ── ✅ THE CONTRADICTION A READER WILL EXPECT, AND WHY THERE ISN'T ONE ───────
# kit.beh's `BEH-WRITE-5` says "A write route is unreachable unless the server
# is bound to loopback" — and hosted Kit is, by definition, not on loopback. It
# reads like a straight conflict, and Kit would not catch it: the symbol table
# is per-corpus, so nothing here can conflict with anything there.
#
# It is not a conflict, because of his own qualification on the C# decision:
# "maybe we can keep node around for now for local dev as an option". The Node
# server keeps serving local dev and BEH-WRITE-5 stays true OF IT. This corpus
# describes a different deployment of a different server, whose write gate is
# `BEH-GATE-*` below rather than the interface it binds. Two apps, two answers,
# both true. That is why nobody has to reach for a `supersede` keyword — which
# does not exist, and whose addition would be a change to the corpus LANGUAGE
# and therefore his (claude-code-bot#58).
# ⚠️ The Node server was deleted in kit#179 (2026-10-09), so the "local dev"
# half of that argument no longer has a server to be true of.
#
# ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# * **No caching behaviour.** I proposed a per-repo shallow clone on kit#88 and
#   he deferred it in the same breath he raised it: "We will see how it goes
#   pulling and pushing files from github without any volume for this pod. In
#   the future we can talk about optimising this process if need be." So the
#   spec says nothing about it. A behaviour asserting a cache would be me
#   deciding something he explicitly postponed.
# * **No language.** Nothing below says C#, .NET or Node. `kit-interim-dual-runtime`
#   (kit#88, acts 2026-10-03) is open, and answering an open question by
#   building is exactly what this corpus must not do. Every behaviour here is
#   true of the hosted service whichever runtime it ships in — which is also
#   the point of specifying before porting.
# * **No merge.** He wrote "raise a PR (and potentially merge it)". The
#   parenthesis is his own hedge and the merge policy is claude-code-bot#54's,
#   not this app's, so the spec stops at the PR.
#
# Every behaviour traces to a line of kit#88 or to a decision recorded in
# docs/design/process.md. Nothing is `source inferred` without an `asks`
# attached, so an inference cannot pass itself off as something he said.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═══ The read path ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# "The backend can pull the projects and serve the behaviours to the frontend."

behaviour BEH-PULL-1 "The project list comes from GitHub, not from the pod's working tree"
  layer technical
  pending
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  given repo:Remote
  when runs command:KitServe
  then reports origin:GitHub
  contract today the list is readdirSync(behaviours/*.beh) against the checkout
  contract the process happens to sit in — correct for a laptop, meaningless for a
  contract pod whose clone is an implementation detail of its image

behaviour BEH-PULL-2 "A public project's behaviours are served without a token, on a request of their own"
  layer technical
  pending
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  given project:Public
  when runs command:KitServe
  then reports auth:NotRequired
  contract his instinct, quoted: "obviously beh from public repos dont need to be
  contract authenticated so maybe can be returned faster" — so the split is not a
  contract performance tweak layered on later, it is which credential the request needs
  contract a public read that fails because a token could not be minted is a bug

behaviour BEH-PULL-3 "A private project is served only against a live installation token"
  layer technical
  pending
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  given project:Private
  when runs command:KitServe
  then reports auth:InstallationToken
  contract the kit-github secret he created on 2026-09-27 holds an app id, an
  contract installation id and a private key — a credential that is EXCHANGED for a
  contract short-lived token, never sent as one
  contract an expired token is re-minted rather than returned as a failed read, which
  contract is what he meant by "some similar refresh token mechanism to what you are using"

behaviour BEH-PULL-4 "The backend parses, resolves and generates; the frontend renders what it is handed"
  layer technical
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  given request:Project
  when runs command:KitServe
  then reports work:ServerSide
  contract "I am envisioning, the frontend has the UI, keep it light let the backend
  contract do the heavy lifting" — so the generated test a screen shows is computed
  contract server-side and arrives as data; the browser never carries the engine
  contract this is also what makes the engine's language a private decision rather
  contract than a public one


# ═══ Acting on a project ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# "Then the user can act on a project, add new behaviours, update behaviours,
# remove behaviours etc and then can 'Commit'."

behaviour BEH-ACT-1 "A behaviour is added to a project from a phone"
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitHostedProject
  then sees button:KitHostedAddBehaviour
  contract "top priority now I think is deploying kit so that I can use it from my
  contract phone" — the phone is the requirement, not a nice-to-have, and it is why
  contract nothing here may depend on a loopback bind

behaviour BEH-ACT-2 "An existing behaviour is updated without rewriting the file around it"
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitHostedBehaviour
  then sees button:KitHostedSave
  contract writer.js already splices one block so every comment survives
  contract (kit.beh BEH-WRITE-1); hosted Kit must keep that property, because the
  contract headers in this directory are where a corpus's caveats live

behaviour BEH-ACT-3 "A behaviour is removed, and the removal is refused if anything still references it"
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitHostedBehaviour
  then sees button:KitHostedRemove
  contract ⚠️ THE ONLY ONE OF HIS THREE VERBS THAT HAS NO IMPLEMENTATION AT ALL —
  contract writer.js can add a step and splice a block, and has no remove path
  contract a behaviour can be the target of `serves` or `cites` from another one, so
  contract deleting it silently is how a corpus acquires a dangling reference

# Reworded 2026-10-10 to the flow that shipped (claude-code-bot#105 spec audit). It
# first said edits were HELD until Commit; what was built pushes each edit to the
# edits branch as it is made — the "write each edit straight through" option this
# block used to weigh, which is the only one a pod with no volume can keep. The
# property his sentence wanted survives: nothing reaches dev until Commit's pull
# request is merged.
behaviour BEH-ACT-4 "Each edit is pushed to the edits branch as it is made, and reaches dev only through Commit"
  source inferred kit#88
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitHostedProject
  then sees button:KitHostedCommit
  contract every write is committed and pushed to kit/hosted at once, so a pod restart between edits loses nothing
  contract no edit is ever committed onto dev; Commit proposes kit/hosted to dev as ONE pull request
  contract a change described over several edits arrives as several commits in that one pull request, not as several branches


# ═══ Commit ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# "The backend can then create a new commit in a branch off develop? Here the AI
# (you for now) can read the behaviour changes, update the generated tests,
# update inferences etc, implement changes in the app to get the tests passing -
# raise a PR (and potentially merge it)."

behaviour BEH-COMMIT-1 "Commit creates a branch off dev, and never a commit on dev"
  layer technical
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  given change:Pending
  when runs command:KitCommit
  then reports branch:Created
  contract answering the question mark in his own sentence: it has to be a branch
  contract a spec written before its code exists makes check.js exit 1 BY DESIGN
  contract (kit.beh BEH-GATE-1), so committing to dev would push a known-red tree
  contract onto the branch every other PR is measured against
  contract ⚠️ UNBUILT: git-store.js defaults to `opts.branch || currentBranch(cwd)`,
  contract so today a pod would push a phone edit onto whatever branch its clone sits on

behaviour BEH-COMMIT-2 "The commit is attributed to Kit, not to the person who pressed Commit"
  layer technical
  source defined docs/design/process.md#3
  actor engineer
  given change:Pending
  when runs command:KitCommit
  then reports author:Kit
  contract his override on kit#88, 2026-09-30: "Let's let the commit belong to kit
  contract for now… using the gh app speeds up timelines"
  contract ⚠️ THIS IS A TRADE HE MADE KNOWINGLY, NOT A LIMITATION — commit
  contract attribution was the argument that carried the earlier web-flow decision,
  contract and he judged shipping sooner worth more while Kit has one user
  contract the condition for revisiting it is a SECOND USER, not a date

behaviour BEH-COMMIT-3 "A pull request is opened for the branch, because that is where the work is picked up"
  source defined kit#88
  actor engineer
  given branch:Pushed
  when runs command:KitCommit
  then reports pull:Opened
  contract "Here the AI (you for now) can read the behaviour changes, update the
  contract generated tests, update inferences etc, implement changes in the app to get
  contract the tests passing - raise a PR"
  contract the PR is the handover: a pushed branch with no PR is invisible to every
  contract instrument that looks for work

behaviour BEH-COMMIT-4 "The branch a Commit produces is red, and the red is the work item"
  layer technical
  source inferred kit#88
  actor engineer
  given branch:Pushed
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:One
  serves BEH-COMMIT-3
  contract MEASURED on 2026-09-30, on a throwaway copy of this corpus directory:
  contract adding one unimplemented behaviour took `20/20 behaviour(s) have a test
  contract naming them` to `20/21` and exit 0 to exit 1
  contract so "the tests are failing" is the normal state of a fresh Kit branch, and
  contract anything that treats a red branch as a fault will fight the workflow he
  contract described rather than serve it


# ═══ The deployed boundary ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# What changes the moment Kit is reachable from a phone rather than from
# localhost. His words: "Since I have set up the secret you should now be able
# to set up kit as a proper full stack app."

behaviour BEH-GATE-H1 "A read is served to anyone, and that stays true in every configuration"
  layer technical
  source defined docs/design/process.md
  actor engineer
  given request:Anonymous
  when runs command:KitServe
  then reports status:Ok
  contract kit is a public repo, so a corpus served to an unauthenticated caller
  contract discloses nothing that `git clone` does not
  contract gating reads would cost the thing he wants — glancing at a spec from a
  contract phone — to protect data that is already public

behaviour BEH-GATE-H2 "A write is refused unless the caller proved they are the operator"
  layer technical
  source defined docs/design/process.md
  actor engineer
  given request:Unauthenticated
  when runs command:KitCommit
  then reports status:Forbidden
  contract this is the half BEH-WRITE-5 used to hold by binding loopback, which a
  contract hosted service cannot do — so the gate moves from the interface to a
  contract credential, and kit#47's KIT_PASSWORD is the first one
  contract ⚠️ TWO DIFFERENT LAYERS, and a later reader must not collapse them: the
  contract App installation token is how KIT talks to GITHUB; the password is how a
  contract PERSON is let into Kit. Neither substitutes for the other
